"""Rheumatoid arthritis: disease modification and treatment decisions."""
from create_rheum_ra_diagnosis import rows,q,C,E,A,I,ref
M=ref('ACR: methotrexate safety and use','https://rheumatology.org/patients/methotrexate-rheumatrex-trexall-otrexup-rasuvo')
B=ref('BSR 2025 guideline for prescribing and monitoring conventional DMARDs','https://doi.org/10.1093/rheumatology/keaf522')
P=ref('EULAR antirheumatic drugs in reproduction, pregnancy and lactation: 2024 update','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40287311/')

q('An otherwise suitable DMARD-naive patient has active RA with moderate-to-high disease activity. Which drug is the usual anchor of the initial conventional DMARD strategy?', 'Methotrexate',[
 ('Hydroxychloroquine alone','It is useful in selected milder disease, but is not generally the preferred anchor for this activity level.'),('Azathioprine','It has selected uses in autoimmune disease but is not the usual initial RA anchor.'),('Cyclophosphamide','Its toxicity and indications do not fit routine initial articular RA treatment.')],
 'Start disease modification early','**Methotrexate is the usual anchor for active RA when there is no contraindication.**',['Assess renal and hepatic function, pregnancy plans, blood counts and infection risks before prescribing.','The weekly regimen and folate supplementation need explicit counseling.'], '**Choose a strategy aimed at sustained disease control.**',['NSAIDs may relieve symptoms but do not replace a DMARD.','Shared decisions should consider comorbidities, access and patient preferences.'],[['Methotrexate','Usual anchor for moderate/high activity'],['Hydroxychloroquine','Often useful in lower activity'],['NSAID','Symptomatic benefit']],[A,M])

q('Which schedule is appropriate for low-dose methotrexate used to treat RA?', 'A prescribed dose once weekly, with an appropriate folate supplementation plan',[
 ('The weekly dose repeated every day','Daily administration of a weekly dose can cause life-threatening toxicity.'),('A single annual dose without monitoring','RA dosing is usually weekly and requires clinical and laboratory follow-up.'),('Only an occasional dose during each painful flare','Methotrexate is a maintenance DMARD, not an immediate as-needed analgesic.')],
 'Prevent the daily-versus-weekly prescribing error','**Low-dose RA methotrexate is taken weekly, not daily.**',['Write the day of the week clearly and review tablet strengths and total dose.','Folate reduces selected adverse effects; schedules vary by local protocol.'], '**Teach the patient to report mouth ulcers, infection, bruising or new breathlessness.**',['Monitor blood counts, liver enzymes and renal function.','If daily overdosing occurs, stop further dosing and arrange urgent toxicity assessment.'],[['RA schedule','Weekly'],['Folate','Reduces selected adverse effects'],['Monitoring','Marrow, liver and renal safety']],[M,B])

q('A patient has persistent active RA despite an adequate trial of oral methotrexate and troublesome nausea. Before abandoning methotrexate, which strategy is reasonable?', 'Review folate and dose tolerability, and consider split oral dosing or subcutaneous methotrexate',[
 ('Add long-term high-dose prednisone without adjusting disease modification','This increases toxicity and does not address the inadequate DMARD strategy.'),('Change methotrexate to the same total dose every day','This creates a dangerous dosing error.'),('Add a second biologic immediately while keeping the intolerable regimen unchanged','Concurrent biologics are generally avoided because of infection risk; methotrexate optimization is a more appropriate step.')],
 'Optimize a useful anchor before declaring failure','**Route and administration changes can improve methotrexate tolerability or exposure.**',['Confirm adherence and that the dose and duration were adequate.','Folate adjustment, split oral dosing within the weekly schedule or subcutaneous administration may help selected patients.'], '**Reassess objective inflammation and treatment goals.**',['Escalation is appropriate when disease remains active despite an optimized strategy.','Do not confuse a weekly split dose with daily long-term use.'],[['Oral intolerance','Review folate and administration'],['Subcutaneous route','May improve exposure/tolerability'],['Persistent activity','Consider treatment escalation']],[A,M],style='clinical_scenario')

q('What does a treat-to-target approach in RA require?', 'Repeated objective assessment and treatment adjustment toward remission or low disease activity',[
 ('Adjustment based only on RF titers','RF does not adequately quantify current inflammatory activity.'),('Continuing the initial treatment indefinitely whenever pain improves slightly','Residual inflammatory activity can continue to cause damage.'),('Escalating immunosuppression for every symptom without assessing its cause','Pain can reflect damage, osteoarthritis, neuropathy or fibromyalgia rather than active synovitis.')],
 'Separate the inflammatory target from symptoms alone','**The treatment target is sustained remission or, when appropriate, low disease activity.**',['Use a validated composite measure together with clinical judgment.','Distinguish persistent synovitis from noninflammatory causes of pain before escalation.'], '**Review frequently during active disease.**',['Check adherence and tolerability alongside joint examination.','The chosen target should reflect comorbidities and the feasibility of treatment.'],[['Swollen joints','Suggest active inflammation'],['Damage-related pain','May need rehabilitation or other treatment'],['Serial assessment','Guides adjustment']],[E,A])

q('According to the EULAR 2025 RA update, published in 2026, what is an appropriate next strategy after insufficient response to the initial methotrexate strategy within 3–6 months?', 'Add a biologic DMARD; consider a JAK inhibitor only after careful individualized risk assessment',[
 ('Require radiographic erosions before any escalation','Treatment aims to prevent damage rather than wait for it.'),('Replace the DMARD with NSAID monotherapy','Symptomatic treatment does not adequately substitute for disease modification.'),('Add two biologic DMARDs together as the routine next step','Concurrent biologics increase safety concerns and are not the standard escalation strategy.')],
 'Use the current escalation framework','**The updated EULAR strategy supports adding a biologic after inadequate initial response.**',['JAK inhibitors remain options after explicit consideration of relevant safety risks.','Evaluate whether persistent symptoms actually reflect active inflammatory disease.'], '**Choose the agent with the patient, accounting for comorbidities.**',['Screen for relevant infections and review vaccination before advanced therapy.','Do not simply prolong glucocorticoids as a substitute for achieving the DMARD target.'],[['Inadequate initial response','Escalate disease modification'],['Biologic selection','Comorbidity and patient factors'],['JAK option','Specific safety discussion required']],[E])

q('Which statement best describes glucocorticoids in the EULAR RA treatment strategy?', 'If used as bridging therapy, use a short course and taper/discontinue rapidly as clinically feasible',[
 ('They should routinely replace all DMARDs for years','Long-term glucocorticoid dependence adds major harm and does not represent optimal disease modification.'),('They must be continued until every autoantibody becomes negative','Autoantibody negativity is not the treatment target.'),('A high daily dose is the preferred permanent maintenance treatment once a biologic starts','Advanced therapy should reduce the need for chronic glucocorticoid exposure.')],
 'Bridge inflammation without creating chronic steroid dependence','**Glucocorticoid exposure should be minimized.**',['EULAR permits short-term bridging with an initial csDMARD strategy; ACR is more cautious about routine use.','Persistent steroid need should trigger review of disease control and alternative causes of symptoms.'], '**Account for infection, fracture, metabolic and cardiovascular risks.**',['Tapering after prolonged use also requires consideration of adrenal suppression.','Use appropriate bone and infection prevention when exposure warrants it.'],[['Bridge','Temporary inflammation control'],['DMARD','Long-term disease modification'],['Persistent steroid requirement','Reassess the treatment strategy']],[E,A])

q('A 69-year-old smoker with RA, prior myocardial infarction and previous DVT is considering tofacitinib. Which issue most directly changes the risk discussion?', 'Increased concerns about major cardiovascular events, malignancy, thrombosis and mortality',[
 ('A uniquely high risk of retinal deposition shared by all JAK inhibitors','Retinal toxicity is classically associated with other medications, notably hydroxychloroquine.'),('A lower thrombotic risk that removes the need to consider his DVT history','Prior thrombosis remains relevant to individual risk assessment.'),('An absolute absence of infection risk because the treatment is oral','Oral targeted therapy can still cause serious and opportunistic infections.')],
 'Do not equate an oral targeted drug with low systemic risk','**JAK inhibitor selection requires a specific benefit-risk discussion in patients with cardiovascular or thrombotic risk.**',['FDA warnings followed a randomized tofacitinib safety trial in an enriched RA population.','Regulatory indications and sequencing requirements vary by jurisdiction.'], '**Compare suitable alternatives rather than choosing solely by route.**',['Address smoking and cardiovascular risk factors.','Review malignancy history, infection risk and vaccination status.'],[['Prior MI/smoking','Raises cardiovascular concern'],['Prior DVT','Relevant thrombosis history'],['Oral administration','Does not eliminate systemic adverse effects']],[ref('FDA JAK inhibitor safety communication','https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-requires-warnings-about-increased-risk-serious-heart-related-events-cancer-blood-clots-and-death')],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Which RA drug directly blocks the IL-6 receptor?', 'Tocilizumab',[
 ('Abatacept','It modulates T-cell costimulation through the CD80/86-CD28 pathway.'),('Rituximab','It targets CD20-positive B cells.'),('Etanercept','It is a soluble TNF receptor fusion protein.')],
 'Match the biological target to the drug','**Tocilizumab blocks IL-6 receptor signaling.**',['IL-6 influences hepatic acute-phase protein production as well as immune inflammation.','CRP responses can therefore be blunted during therapy, including during infection.'], '**Interpret a low CRP with the clinical examination rather than as a guarantee of safety.**',['Monitor blood counts, liver enzymes and lipids as recommended.','Abdominal symptoms warrant assessment, particularly with diverticular disease or other perforation risks.'],[['Tocilizumab','IL-6 receptor'],['Abatacept','T-cell costimulation'],['Rituximab','CD20 B cells']],[ref('FDA prescribing information: Actemra','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/125276s134lbl.pdf')])

q('Which mechanism best describes abatacept?', 'CTLA4-Ig binding CD80/86 and reducing CD28-mediated T-cell costimulation',[
 ('Selective depletion of CD20-positive B cells','This describes rituximab.'),('Neutralization of IL-17A','This describes agents used in other inflammatory diseases, not abatacept.'),('Intracellular Janus kinase inhibition','This describes targeted synthetic JAK inhibitors.')],
 'Costimulation differs from cytokine blockade','**Abatacept interferes with a second signal needed for effective T-cell activation.**',['It is a biologic DMARD rather than a JAK inhibitor.','Treatment selection still requires infection and comorbidity review.'], '**Distinguish targets when sequencing advanced treatments.**',['Failure of one pathway does not establish that all mechanisms will fail.','Avoid simultaneous use with another biologic as routine RA treatment.'],[['Abatacept','Costimulation modulation'],['Rituximab','B-cell depletion'],['JAK inhibitor','Intracellular signaling inhibition']],[ref('Orencia US prescribing information','https://packageinserts.bms.com/pi/pi_orencia.pdf')])

q('Which advanced RA treatment may be favored in a patient with a previous lymphoproliferative disorder for which rituximab is an approved treatment?', 'Rituximab',[
 ('A TNF inhibitor automatically regardless of the prior malignancy','The history changes drug selection; it should not be ignored.'),('Tofacitinib solely because it is oral','Route does not overcome malignancy-related safety concerns.'),('Simultaneous rituximab and a TNF inhibitor','Combining biologics is not the recommended way to address this scenario.')],
 'Use the comorbidity to guide the mechanism','**ACR conditionally favors rituximab in this specific prior lymphoproliferative setting.**',['This is a context-specific recommendation, not a rule for every previous cancer.','Coordinate with oncology and consider disease status, prior therapy and infection risk.'], '**Assess hepatitis B status and immunoglobulins where appropriate before B-cell depletion.**',['Rituximab can reactivate HBV, including in some anti-HBc-positive patients.','Vaccination planning is important because antibody responses may be reduced after treatment.'],[['Prior rituximab-responsive lymphoproliferative disease','Rituximab may be favored'],['Other cancer histories','Require individualized assessment'],['Concurrent biologics','Generally avoided']],[A],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A patient with active RA has NYHA class III heart failure. Which advanced-treatment principle is preferred in the ACR guideline?', 'Prefer a suitable non-TNF biologic or targeted synthetic DMARD over a TNF inhibitor, with individual safety assessment',[
 ('Prefer a TNF inhibitor because TNF blockade reliably improves advanced heart failure','Trials in heart failure raised worsening concerns rather than establishing this benefit.'),('Use high-dose prednisone as permanent treatment because all DMARDs are contraindicated','Heart failure does not eliminate all disease-modifying options.'),('Combine two TNF inhibitors at low doses','There is no standard role for this combination.')],
 'Heart failure changes RA drug selection','**Advanced symptomatic heart failure is a reason to favor a non-TNF approach.**',['The recommendation is conditional and agent selection must still consider other risks.','A JAK inhibitor is not automatically the safest alternative in a patient with cardiovascular disease.'], '**Coordinate disease control with cardiac care.**',['Review fluid-retaining and cardiovascular effects of symptomatic medications.','Reassess treatment if heart failure develops during TNF inhibitor use.'],[['NYHA III/IV heart failure','Prefer a non-TNF strategy'],['JAK alternative','Assess its separate safety profile'],['NSAIDs/steroids','Can add cardiovascular or fluid-related harm']],[A],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A patient has sustained RA remission on a DMARD regimen and asks to stop all treatment. Which principle is most appropriate?', 'Discuss cautious dose reduction after sustained remission, recognizing that complete cessation commonly risks flare',[
 ('Stop every DMARD immediately because remission proves permanent cure','RA can relapse after withdrawal.'),('Taper despite residual swollen joints whenever the ESR is normal','Clinical synovitis argues against stable inflammatory remission.'),('Use intermittent high-dose glucocorticoids instead of maintenance DMARDs','This substitutes recurrent toxicity for a durable disease-control strategy.')],
 'Remission permits discussion, not an assumption of cure','**Tapering is individualized after sustained disease control.**',['Assess duration of remission, prior flares, damage and patient preferences.','The plan should specify monitoring and prompt action if activity returns.'], '**Reduce treatment cautiously rather than leave inflammation unmonitored.**',['Different agents have different dose-spacing and withdrawal options.','Distinguish medication adverse effects from an otherwise stable remission before changing the regimen.'],[['Sustained remission','Consider cautious taper'],['Complete cessation','Substantial relapse risk'],['New synovitis','Reassess and restore control']],[E,A],style='clinical_scenario')

if __name__=='__main__':print(f'Rheumatoid arthritis cumulative questions: {len(rows)}')
